KLIN TLS KEY MANAGER
--------------------

The private key of an HTTPS server is kept out of the processes that
serve requests. At startup, before any worker is forked, klind starts a
key manager, a small process of its own, which reads the key file itself
and tells klind only the public key: klind never decodes the private
key. Workers (and klind itself) only hold a proxy key: when
a TLS handshake needs a signature, the worker sends the digest to the key
manager, which signs it and sends the signature back. A worker that runs
attacker-controlled code can ask for signatures while it runs, but it
cannot read the key, and the key does not leave with a memory dump or a
core file of a worker.

It is on by default for every HTTPS server; to keep the key in klind's
processes, as before 4.0.0:

    secure
    {
        type        https
        cert_file   /etc/klin/cert.pem
        key_file    /etc/klin/key.pem
        key_manager no
    }

HOW IT WORKS

- One key manager per HTTPS server. It closes every descriptor but its
  control socket, ignores HUP and ALRM, and, when klind runs as root with
  uid and gid set, drops to them like klind does.
- The proxy key is an OpenSSL 3 provider built into klind
  ("klin-keyproxy"): its key management answers with the public key
  (certificate checks, sizes, curve), its signature operation forwards
  the digest and the RSA padding, PSS salt length and MGF1 digest. RSA
  (PKCS #1 v1.5 and PSS, TLS 1.2 and 1.3) and EC keys (ECDSA) are
  supported; other key types stop klind at startup (set key_manager no).
- Each signature: the worker creates a socket pair, sends the request and
  one end of the pair over the control socket every klind process
  inherited, and waits up to 5 s for the answer on the other end.
- TLS 1.2 RSA key exchange is turned off ("DEFAULT:!kRSA"): it decrypts
  with the private key, which the key manager does not do, and it has no
  forward secrecy. Every current client uses ECDHE.
- If a key manager exits, klind stops: nothing else holds the key, so no
  handshake could succeed. A key manager exits when klind does.
- Key and certificate files are read into OpenSSL secure memory (wiped
  when freed), not through stdio. Until 4.0's last changes klind decoded
  the key and handed it over; OpenSSL's decoder leaves copies in ordinary
  freed memory, which the workers inherited (seen with two servers
  sharing a key file, the second with a ca_file). The key manager now
  reads the file, so klind's memory never has the key.
- One key manager per HTTPS server: servers sharing a key file have one
  each.

LIMITS

- A key embedded in klind (a key_file in the site, see the import) is in
  every process's memory as part of the program: klind warns at startup.
  Keep keys in files outside the site.
- The key manager is a signing oracle for the workers while they run: it
  protects the key, not the TLS sessions of a compromised worker.
- Native Windows has no key manager.

The server tests check it: after TLS requests they scan the memory of
klind and every process it started (Linux, /proc) for the private key,
and expect to find it in the key managers only (two servers share the
key, one of them with client certificates: the case that leaked).
